        <div id="incline-and-speed" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. 40% Incline, -6% Decline and the Speed Limits</h2>
<p>
            Forty percent is the X24’s reason to exist. A grade that steep turns a walk into a climb:
            effort rises steeply while impact stays at walking levels, which is why incline trainers
            appeal to hikers preparing for mountain trips, to runners who want hard aerobic work
            without the pounding, and to anyone who finds running uncomfortable. Our guide to
            <a href="/incline-trainer-vs-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">incline trainers vs treadmills</a>
            sets out who benefits, and our
            <a href="/treadmills-with-the-highest-incline/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">highest-incline treadmills</a>
            guide compares the X24 with the few machines that go as steep.
          </p>
          <p>
            Treadmill Review Guru measured the X24 at 39.4% when set to 40% and at -4.9% when set to
            -6%, and found the "flat" setting sat at about 1%. Those are normal tolerances for a home
            treadmill. More important is what it found about speed. In its testing the top speed was
            capped at 8 mph once the grade passed 16%, and at 6 mph from 25% upward. On the downhill
            side, speed was capped at 8 mph at -3% and 6 mph at -6%. Run to the Finish found the 40%
            setting far more demanding than the 1750’s incline range and the decline useful for
            training downhill muscles.
          </p>
          <p>
            In practice, the caps matter little for the training the X24 is built for: almost nobody
            runs at 8 mph up a 20% grade, and steep incline work is walking. But they mean the X24 is
            not a better running treadmill than a NordicTrack Commercial model or a Sole; it is a
            running treadmill at ordinary grades and a climbing machine at steep ones. If your plan is
            fast running with occasional hills, a machine with 12 to 15% incline does the job for far
            less. If your plan is steep walking, start lower than you think: our guide to
            <a href="/what-incline-should-i-walk-on-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">what incline to walk at</a>
            explains how to build up, and the
            <a href="/12-3-30-treadmill-workout/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">12-3-30 workout</a>
            uses barely a third of the X24’s range. Our guide to
            <a href="/what-is-treadmill-decline-good-for/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill decline</a>
            covers the downhill side.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="motor-and-deck" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. Motor, Deck and Build</h2>
<p>
            NordicTrack rates the X24’s motor at 4.25 CHP, which it describes as a lab-measured
            continuous rating, and covers it for life. Treadmill Review Guru timed 1 to 12 mph at about
            15 seconds, faster than the <a href="/peloton-tread/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>Peloton Tread</a> and the Horizon models it had tested and about a
            second slower than the NordicTrack Commercial 2450. That is quick, and it matters for
            interval sessions at running grades. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-horsepower-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">horsepower guide</a>
            explains continuous and peak ratings.
          </p>
          <p>
            The belt is 22 by 60 inches, the size most runners should look for, on NordicTrack’s
            SpringFlex cushioning. Treadmill Review Guru found the deck softer than most brands it has
            tested but firmer than the older X32i and X22i, and more consistent at speed; Run to the
            Finish describes it as solid with little flex even at steep grades, and comfortable for
            long sessions. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-cushioning-explained/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">cushioning guide</a>
            explains what deck cushioning does and does not do for joints.
          </p>
          <p>
            The 400 lb rating is among the highest on a home treadmill, and with the wide belt and
            non-folding frame it makes the X24 a reasonable choice for heavier users who want incline
            walking above all; our
            <a href="/best-treadmills-for-heavy-people/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmills for heavy people</a>
            guide compares capacities. The price of that build is weight: 445 lb in the box, and a
            machine that does not fold and is hard to move once installed.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="screen-and-ifit" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. The 24-Inch Screen and What iFIT Costs</h2>
<p>
            The 24-inch touchscreen pivots, so you can turn it to follow strength, yoga or stretching
            classes on the floor beside the machine. Treadmill Review Guru describes it as easy to reach
            and see; Run to the Finish found it bright and responsive with occasional lag, and the
            first review also noted glare on the dark interface. Both note what the screen is for:
            iFIT. With a membership, iFIT trainer-led and filmed-route workouts change the speed and
            grade automatically, and iFIT’s SmartAdjust and ActivePulse features adjust sessions to
            your performance and heart rate. Treadmill Review Guru also reports Spotify and Netflix
            access through iFIT, with a subscription.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The membership</h3>
          <p>
            NordicTrack’s checkout page says an iFIT Pro membership is required for full content and
            features on the X24, that it auto-renews at $39 a month plus tax unless cancelled, and that
            choosing a one-, two- or three-year iFIT Pro plan at checkout takes $100 off the order. Over
            three years, $39 a month is about $1,400, which is a third of the machine’s checkout price
            again; budget for it, or plan how you will use the machine without it. Our
            <a href="/can-you-use-nordictrack-without-ifit/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">guide to using a NordicTrack without iFIT</a>
            sets out what manual mode gives you, and
            <a href="/is-ifit-worth-it/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">is iFIT worth it?</a>
            weighs the membership honestly.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Controls</h3>
          <p>
            One complaint in Treadmill Review Guru’s review deserves attention if you will run on the
            X24 at speed: it found no quick-touch speed and incline buttons on the console, so
            adjustments are made on the screen, which it found hard to use while running. It also
            noted no device rack and no front handle. Run to the Finish found the handlebars slick
            with sweaty hands. None of these matters much for steep walking, where you change grade
            slowly; they matter more for intervals.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="owner-reports" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. What Independent Reviews Consistently Report</h2>
<p>
            We list a point here only when it appears in both of the reviews we read, or when one
            review measured it.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>A stable, solid deck at steep grades.</strong> Both reviews describe the deck as stable and well cushioned; neither reports flex at 40%.</li>
            <li><strong>Large, heavy and expensive.</strong> Both list size, weight and price among the cons, and both note it does not fold.</li>
            <li><strong>Best features need iFIT.</strong> Both say the screen’s value depends on a paid membership.</li>
            <li><strong>Screen niggles.</strong> One reports occasional touchscreen lag, the other glare on the dark interface.</li>
            <li><strong>Noise.</strong> Run to the Finish found it quiet for its size, with the incline motor audible during grade changes; Treadmill Review Guru found it louder than the quietest treadmills it has tested, like a kitchen exhaust fan on high at speed.</li>
            <li><strong>Speed caps at steep grades.</strong> Measured by one review, as described above.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Treadmill Review Guru notes that NordicTrack supplied its test machine without paying for
            the review. Our guide to
            <a href="/how-to-read-treadmill-reviews/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">reading treadmill reviews</a>
            explains why that is worth knowing and how to weigh single reports.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="space-ceiling-delivery" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Space, Ceiling Height, Power and Delivery</h2>
<p>
            In use the X24 is 69.9 inches long, 38.1 wide and 73.3 tall, about 18.5 square feet, and it
            stays that way because it does not fold. Boxed it is 77 by 42 by 34 inches and 445 lb, so
            check every doorway, corner and stair on the route in before you order. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-dimensions-space-requirements/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">space guide</a>
            covers clearance behind the deck.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Ceiling height</h3>
          <p>
            This is where incline trainers catch buyers out. NordicTrack lists a 13.66-inch step-up
            height, and Treadmill Review Guru measured the deck at 14 inches flat and 31.5 inches at
            full incline. Run to the Finish says NordicTrack suggests the user’s height plus three feet
            as a minimum ceiling. For a six-foot user that is nine feet. Measure from the floor to the
            lowest beam, duct or light above where you will stand, and use the tallest person in the
            house. Most basements fail that test, and it is the single most common reason an incline
            trainer cannot go where its buyer planned.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Power and delivery</h3>
          <p>
            Plan on a dedicated, grounded circuit and no extension lead or surge strip; our
            <a href="/treadmill-extension-cord/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">circuit guide</a>
            explains why. NordicTrack’s checkout page lists threshold, room-of-choice and white-glove
            delivery and assembly, with professional installation depending on zip code, but showed
            no prices for them on the day; it estimated delivery at 10 to 14 business days and
            mentioned free shipping subject to terms. Treadmill Review Guru says assembly needs at
            least two people. On a 445 lb machine, white-glove assembly is worth pricing before you
            decide.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="price-and-warranty" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Price, Warranty and Safety Record</h2>
<p>
            On 5 November 2026 NordicTrack’s checkout page listed the X24 at $4,299 and in stock.
            NordicTrack’s main product pages could not be read on the day, and search results for them
            showed both $2,999.99 and $4,299.99, plus a $2,499.99 trade-up price that depends on a
            mailed code and an $1,800 credit. We could not confirm any of the lower figures, so treat
            $4,299 as a ceiling and expect promotions. With Black Friday on 27 November, our
            <a href="/nordictrack-black-friday-deals/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">NordicTrack Black Friday guide</a>
            sets out how NordicTrack’s sale pricing works, and our
            <a href="/treadmill-black-friday-deals/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Black Friday method page</a>
            explains how to judge a discount against a list price.
          </p>
          <p>
            The included warranty is a lifetime on the motor, ten years on the frame, two on parts and
            one on labour, and NordicTrack offers a three-year extended plan that raises those to
            13, 5 and 4 years, and a separate three-year maintenance plan with one in-home check a
            year. Two years of parts on a $4,299 machine with a motorised deck that tilts through 46
            percentage points is short; if you buy the X24, price the extended plan, because the
            incline mechanism is the part an incline trainer works hardest. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-warranty-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">warranty guide</a>
            explains how to judge one.
          </p>
          <p>
            Safety record: we searched the US Consumer Product Safety Commission’s recall database on
            5 November 2026 for NordicTrack and its parent company, iFIT, and found no NordicTrack
            treadmill recall. The recent recalls were of the NordicTrack RW900 <a href="/best-rowing-machines/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>rowing machine</a> (October
            2025, fire hazard) and 50 lb iSelect adjustable dumbbells (February 2024). Our
            <a href="/nordictrack/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">NordicTrack brand guide</a>
            covers the company and its service record.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="vs-x16" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. NordicTrack X24 vs X16, X22i and Commercial 2450</h2>
<p>
            Prices below are those shown on NordicTrack’s checkout pages on 5 November 2026.
          </p>
