          </table></div>
          <p>
            <strong>X24 vs X16.</strong> The decision is unusually clean: everything that determines
            how the machine trains, lasts and feels underfoot is shared, and the $600 difference buys
            eight inches of screen. Pay it if you will follow iFIT classes every session, especially
            floor classes with the screen turned, and the larger picture is what keeps you on the
            machine. Otherwise buy the X16 and put the difference towards the first year of iFIT or
            the extended warranty.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>X22i.</strong> The X24 and X16 have taken the place of the X22i and X32i on
            NordicTrack’s site. The X22i had a 22-inch screen and a similarly steep incline range; if
            you are looking at a discounted or second-hand one, our
            <a href="/nordictrack-x22i-incline-trainer-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">X22i review</a>
            covers it, and the
            <a href="/used-treadmill-buying-checklist/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">used treadmill checklist</a>
            is the place to start. Check that any iFIT features you want are still supported on the
            older console.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Commercial 2450.</strong> If you mostly run and want a big screen, NordicTrack’s
            Commercial 2450 has a 24-inch screen and a conventional incline range and declines to -3%;
            our <a href="/nordictrack-commercial-2450-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Commercial 2450 review</a>
            sets out its figures. The full range is ranked in our
            <a href="/best-nordictrack-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best NordicTrack treadmills</a>
            guide, and machines from other brands in our
            <a href="/best-incline-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best incline treadmills</a>
            roundup.
          </p>
