        <div id="who-should-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. Who Should Buy the NordicTrack X24</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Buy it if steep incline walking is your main training.</strong> Hikers, people
            preparing for mountain trips and anyone who wants hard aerobic work without running get
            more from a 40% deck than from any conventional treadmill.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Buy it if you will use iFIT and want the big screen.</strong> The X24 is the X16
            with a 24-inch display; that is only worth paying for if you will watch it every session.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Buy the X16 instead</strong> if the screen size is not the point. It is the same
            machine for $600 less at the checkout prices we saw.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Look elsewhere</strong> if you mainly run fast, where the speed caps at steep
            grades make the X24’s range less useful than it looks; if your ceiling is under about nine
            feet for a six-foot user; or if you do not want a subscription, where a Horizon or Sole
            without one makes more sense.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Do not pay the list price without checking promotions.</strong> NordicTrack’s
            pricing moves constantly, and Black Friday is three weeks away.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">11. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is the NordicTrack X24 treadmill worth it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For steep incline walking and hiking, yes: it goes to 40% and declines to -6%, with a 4.25 CHP motor, a 400 lb rating and a stable deck in two independent reviews. For running, a cheaper treadmill does as well, because one review found its top speed capped at 8 mph above 16% incline. The X16 is the same machine with a smaller screen for less. We have not tested it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How steep does the NordicTrack X24 go?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                NordicTrack lists a grade range of -6% to 40%. Treadmill Review Guru measured 39.4% at full incline and -4.9% at full decline, and found the top speed capped at 8 mph above 16%, 6 mph from 25%, and 8 mph at -3% and 6 mph at -6% on decline.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does the NordicTrack X24 need iFIT?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                NordicTrack says an iFIT Pro membership is required for full content and features, and that it auto-renews at $39 a month plus tax unless cancelled. The machine can be used in a manual mode without classes, but the automatic speed and grade control that makes the screen worthwhile needs the membership.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">NordicTrack X24 vs X16: what is the difference?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The screen. NordicTrack lists the same 22 by 60-inch belt, 4.25 CHP motor, -6% to 40% grade, 400 lb rating, footprint and warranty for both. The X24 has a 24-inch pivoting screen and the X16 a 16-inch one. On 5 November 2026 NordicTrack’s checkout pages listed the X24 at $4,299 and the X16 at $3,699.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much ceiling height does the NordicTrack X24 need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                NordicTrack lists a 13.66-inch step-up height, and one review measured the deck at 31.5 inches at full incline. Run to the Finish reports NordicTrack’s guidance as the user’s height plus three feet, which is nine feet for a six-foot user. Measure to the lowest beam or duct above the deck.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does the NordicTrack X24 fold?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. It is a non-folding incline trainer measuring 69.9 by 38.1 by 73.3 inches in use, and it weighs 445 lb in the box. Plan the delivery route and its final position before you order.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes Buying a NordicTrack X24</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">The X24 is a specialised machine, and most buying mistakes come from treating it like an ordinary treadmill.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Not measuring the ceiling</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A 13.66-inch step-up height and a deck that rises to about 31.5 inches at full incline need a tall room. Use the tallest user, add three feet as NordicTrack suggests, and measure to the lowest obstruction.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying it as a running treadmill</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Its top speed falls to 8 mph above 16% incline and 6 mph from 25% in one review’s testing. If you mostly run fast, a conventional treadmill with 12 to 15% incline does the job for far less.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Paying for the X24 when the X16 would do</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">The two share every mechanical figure and the warranty. The extra money buys a bigger screen, which is worth it only if you will use iFIT classes every session.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving iFIT out of the budget</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">iFIT Pro auto-renews at $39 a month, about $1,400 over three years. Decide before you buy whether you will pay it, and what you will use the screen for if you do not.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

